In Docker, a network is a virtual communication environment that allows containers to communicate with each other and, depending on its configuration, with external networks.
Docker gives every container its own network namespace — its own IP, gateway, and DNS resolver. 
Networking is about two separate questions: 
- Can container A reach container B? # depends on whether they share a user-defined network.
- Can the host or the outside world reach the container? #depends on whether you publish ports (-p).
These are two different issues, Containers on the same network reach each other on any port without -p & Publishing a port only affects access from the host/outside.


Network Drivers # Network driver = the technology used to create the virtual communication environment defined above.
Docker Engine has a number of network drivers, as well as the default "bridge". On Linux, the following built-in network drivers are available:
Driver                       	What It Does	                                                                                 When to Use
bridge (default)	   Isolated network with NAT. Containers get IPs.	                                Almost everything on a single host. But always use a user-defined bridge, not the default.
host           	Remove network isolation between the container and the Docker host.       Performance-critical apps, or when you need direct access to host interfaces.
                                                                                            Host mode removes isolation—the container can bind any host port and see all host traffic .
none	                   No network at all.	                                                            Batch jobs that need no network, or maximum isolation.
overlay      	Connects multiple Docker daemons across machines.	                                          Docker Swarm services spanning hosts.
macvlan	          Container gets its own MAC address on the LAN.	                                       Legacy apps that expect to be a physical device.
ipvlan	Connect containers to external VLANs.

NOTE:
#When you start containers without specifying a network, Docker normally attaches them to the default bridge network. eg * docker run -d --name frontend nginx
#User-defined bridge network: same bridge network but created by you * docker network create my-network 
   #then you attach containers to it * docker run -d --name frontend --network my-network nginx 
Support note: If a container uses *"network_mode: host" in Compose, service-name DNS does not work. The container is on the host network and cannot resolve other Compose services by name.

Essential Commands and Flags
Create a user-defined network:
*docker network create my-net
#This is the single most important fix for DNS issues between containers.

With options:
*docker network create --driver bridge --subnet 172.28.0.0/16 --gateway 172.28.0.1 --internal my-net
Command / option	                   Meaning
docker network create	      Creates a new Docker network.
--driver bridge           	Uses the bridge network driver.
--subnet 172.28.0.0/16	    Defines the IP address range available to containers.
--gateway 172.28.0.1	      Defines the gateway IP address for that network.
--internal	                Restricts external connectivity for the network.
my-net	                    Name assigned to the network.

Understanding the subnet
172.28.0.0/16 defines an IP address range.
For example, containers connected to this network could receive addresses such as:
- 172.28.0.2 or 172.28.0.3 or 172.28.0.4  #The gateway 172.28.0.1 is the network's gateway address.

What does --internal do?
It creates an internally isolated network. Containers can communicate with each other, but the network does not provide normal external connectivity.
For example, a backend and database could communicate internally without needing internet access.
--internal: Blocks outbound internet access. Useful for a database tier that should not phone home.

Run a container on a network:
* docker run --network my-net --name backend my-image  #Starts a container on that network
   # --network my-net	- Connects the container to my-net.
   # --name backend	Names the container backend.
   # my-image	Image used to create the container.
#The container is connected to my-net from the moment it starts.
       Because this is a user-defined bridge network, other containers on that network can reach it using its name: http://backend:8080
       Assuming the application listens on port 8080.
* docker network connect my-net backend #docker network connect: connects an already-existing container, backend to an additional network.
* docker network connect --alias db my-net my-container # This does the same thing as the previous command, but adds a network-specific DNS alias.
# Other containers on my-net can now reach it using: db
A container can be on multiple networks simultaneously. This is useful for a backend that needs to talk to both a frontend network and an isolated database network.

Inspect
* docker network inspect my-net # Shows which containers are connected, their IPs, and the network's subnet.
Publish a port (inbound access only):
docker run -p 127.0.0.1:8080:8080 my-image  #The 127.0.0.1: prefix binds only to loopback, which is safer for local development. 
 #Plain -p 8080:8080 binds to 0.0.0.0 (all interfaces) and can bypass host firewalls like ufw.
 #For support, if a user says "I can reach it locally but not from another machine," the issue may be that they bound to 127.0.0.1 instead of 0.0.0.0.

Inbound vs. Outbound: Two Different Problems
--Outbound (container → external API/database):
Works by default on bridge networks via NAT. No special flags needed. If it fails, the problem is almost always host-level networking: 
firewall rules blocking outbound traffic, a VPN that changes DNS servers, or a private subnet that the host cannot route to .

Diagnostic sequence for outbound failures:
*docker exec <container> ping 8.8.8.8 #tests raw IP connectivity.
*docker exec <container> cat /etc/resolv.conf #checks what DNS server the container is using. If it shows 127.0.0.53,
  that's the systemd-resolved stub and it won't work from inside the container .
*docker exec <container> nslookup google.com 8.8.8.8 #tests DNS against a known-good server.

--Inbound (outside → container):
Requires -p. The browser on the host reaches the container at localhost:<published_port>. This is the path used for web applications. 
Container-to-container traffic does not use this path.

Compose Networking
Compose creates a default user-defined network for the project automatically. This is why services can reach each other by service name (backend, frontend) out of the box.

Custom networks in Compose:
services:
  frontend:
    networks:
      - app-net
    ports:
      - "127.0.0.1:5001:5001"

  backend:
    networks:
      - app-net
      - db-net
    ports:
      - "127.0.0.1:8080:8080"

networks:
  app-net:
  db-net:
    internal: true
#Services on the same network resolve each other by service name.
#internal: true creates a network with no external connectivity—useful for database tiers .
#A service connected to both an internal and a non-internal network can still reach the internet via the non-internal one .

Important Compose behavior: When you run docker compose up after a configuration change, the old container is removed and,
the new container joins the network with the same name but a different IP. Running containers that have open connections to the old container will have those connections closed. 
They must re-resolve the name and reconnect . This can cause transient failures during deployments—important to know for support.

Support Scenarios and Quick Diagnostics # () IS THE LIKELY CAUSE
- Container A can't reach Container B by name	 (They are on the default bridge, not a user-defined network)	*FIX: Create a user-defined network and attach both containers
- Browser can't reach backend at http://backend:8080	(The browser is not on the Docker network; it needs a host-reachable URL) *FIX: Use http://localhost:8080 (with the backend port published)
- Container can't resolve external hostnames	(Host's /etc/resolv.conf points to 127.0.0.53 (systemd-resolved stub))	*FIX: Configure Docker to use upstream DNS servers in daemon.json, or pass --dns per container 
- Port published but unreachable from other machines	(Bound to 127.0.0.1 instead of 0.0.0.0) *FIX:	Change to -p 8080:8080 (or explicitly bind to the correct interface)
- Compose service can't reach another after up	(Container was recreated with a new IP; cached connections are stale) *FIX:	Restart the dependent service, or ensure the app re-resolves DNS on connection failure

Useful diagnostic commands:
docker exec <container> cat /etc/resolv.conf          # What DNS server is the container using?
docker exec <container> ping -c 2 <other-container>   # Does name resolution work?
docker exec <container> nslookup google.com            # Does external DNS work?
docker network inspect <network>                       # Who is on this network, with what IPs?
docker port <container>                                # What ports are published?

The One-Sentence Summary
Docker networking is two separate problems: 
- container-to-container reachability (solved by putting containers on a user-defined network, which provides automatic DNS by container name)
- host-to-container reachability (solved by publishing ports, ideally bound to 127.0.0.1 for local safety); outbound traffic works by default via NAT,
but browser-based applications must use host-reachable URLs because the browser is never on the Docker network.


###How Docker DNS Works #DNS is where most Docker networking problems occur. 
Docker runs an embedded DNS server at 127.0.0.11 inside every container on a user-defined network . When a container tries to resolve a name (e.g., backend), 
the request goes to this embedded server first.
- On a user-defined bridge network, Docker's embedded DNS resolves container names and network aliases automatically . If you start a container with --name backend, 
any other container on the same network can reach it at http://backend:8080.
- On the default bridge network, there is no automatic DNS for container names. Containers can only reach each other by IP address . 
This is the single most common cause of "container A can't reach container B" issues. The default bridge is considered a legacy detail and is not recommended for production .

For external DNS (resolving google.com or an external API hostname), containers on user-defined networks forward requests to the host's configured DNS servers via the embedded resolver . 
If the host can resolve it, the container usually can too—unless the host uses systemd-resolved with the stub listener 127.0.0.53, which is not reachable from inside containers. 
In that case, you need to configure Docker to use the real upstream DNS servers instead .

The Browser-vs-Container Trap (Critical for Support)
This is the most frequent misunderstanding in full-stack Docker setups, and you will encounter it in support tickets.
When a browser (on the user's machine) accesses a web application served by a container, the browser is not on the Docker network.
It is on the host or on a remote machine entirely. Therefore:
- A service name like http://backend:8080 will not work in the browser. That name only resolves inside the Docker network .
- The browser must use a host-reachable address: http://localhost:8080 (if the backend port is published) or the host's IP/hostname.

The same applies to any JavaScript running in the browser. The frontend container's job is only to serve static files; the code executes in the browser,
and its API calls originate from the user's machine, not from the frontend container .

Rule for support: If a user reports "the frontend can't reach the backend," ask: Is the failing request coming from the browser (visible in DevTools Network tab) or 
from server-side code inside a container? If it's from the browser, the URL must be host-reachable, not a Docker service name.
